  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\nRidging continues to build aloft over the Plains and will move over \nthe area by Wednesday night. A tighter sfc pressure gradient today \nalong with good mixing will lead to breezier conditions with winds \nof 15-20 mph and gusts to around 30 mph. EPS gusts are mainly \ngreater than a 60% chance of gusts over 25 mph. Also lowered \ndewpoints to NBM 10th percentile due to the anticipated mixing. This \ncoupled with the winds will make for lower RH values (25-35%) this \nafternoon and elevated fire danger. Have issued an SPS for elevated \nfire danger. Have also issued a Lake Wind Advisory for the breezy \nconditions. \n\nNBM chances for 80+ degrees increase tomorrow and Thursday. NBM has \ngenerally a 40-60% chance for eastern portions of the area to reach \n80 tomorrow. NBM has a greater than 70% chance of reaching at least \n80 on Thursday across the board. \n\nThe upper ridge pushes east on Friday as a leading shortwave around \na Canadian border low pushes into the Upper Midwest. This system \nbrings a cold front and round of showers/storms to the area as early \nas Friday morning (60-80% chance). Showers last at least till \nSaturday morning. There are model discrepancies over whether or not \nshowers/storms continue on Saturday (depending on frontal \nplacement). NBM currently has a 20-30% chance roughly behind the \nfront (just to our south Saturday). NBM also has generally a 30-60% \nchance of CAPE values greater than 1,000 J/kg in SEMO Friday \nafternoon and generally 30-50% in western portions of southern IL. \nSo a few rogue strong to sever storms can't be ruled out Friday. \nMonday a shortwave aloft brings another sfc low through the Midwest \nand brings another round of showers/storms and another cold front \nthrough the area.",
